<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.1.3: Interpreting the Slope and Vertical Intercept of a Linear Model</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00695</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.1.3: Interpreting the Slope and Vertical Intercept of a Linear Model</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f968d3fa-66b0-4e56-a055-874be5d46e93</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>The scatter plot shows the sale price of several food items, \(y\), and the cost of the ingredients used to produce those items, \(x\), as well as a line that models the data. The line is also represented by the equation \(y = 3.48x + 0.76\).</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="Scatterplot and line of best fit.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_06_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the predicted sale price of an item that has ingredients that cost $1.50? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>$5.98 from the equation \((3.48⋅1.5+0.76=5.98)\), or approximately $6 from the scatter plot graph</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the predicted ingredient cost of an item that has a sale price of $7? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>$1.79 from the equation \(\left(\frac{7-0.76}{3.48}\;=\;1.79\right)\), or approximately $1.75 from the scatter plot graph
  </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the slope of the linear model?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>3.48.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does the slope value mean in this situation?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. This means that for every extra dollar spent on ingredients, the sale price of the food item is expected to increase by about $3.48</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What is the 𝑦-intercept of the linear model?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>0.76</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does the value of the \(y\)-intercept mean in this situation?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. This means that a food item that is made with free ingredients would still be sold for approximately 76 cents.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Does the value of the \(y\)-intercept make sense in this situation?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
          <list>
            <item>This doesn’t seem to make sense because something that is free to make should not cost anything for the customer.</item>
            <item>This might make sense since it would still cost some money to either run the machines or pay the people to make the item.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: Using Linear Models</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to use linear models.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c7081f288d0cb5e03052a1f936334b0b53cd61ce"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
      <para>The scatter plot below shows the age (in years) and the price (in dollars) of used compact cars advertised in the local newspaper.</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="A SCATTER PLOT THAT SHOWS AGE IN YEARS ON THE X-AXIS AND PRICE IN DOLLARS ON THE Y-AXIS. THE LINE DRAWN DECREASES FROM LEFT TO RIGHT AND PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (13, 6000) AND (7, 12000).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_07_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para>Nora drew a line to fit the data. What is the slope of her line, and what does it mean in the problem?</para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section>
      <title>Interpreting a Linear Model</title>
      <para>The scatter plot below shows the height and speed of some of the world’s fastest roller coasters. A line has been drawn to show a good fit for the data. 
  
</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="A SCATTER PLOT THAT SHOWS MAXIMUM HEIGHT IN FEET ON THE X-AXIS AND SPEED IN MILES PER HOUR ON THE Y-AXIS. THE LINE DRAWN INCREASES FROM LEFT TO RIGHT.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para>The equation of the line drawn is \(S= 0.11h +60\).</para>
      <para>What does this slope mean in the problem?</para>
      <para>For every foot taller the roller coaster is, the speed increases 0.11 miles per hour.</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Interpreting a Linear Model</title>
      <para>Eight students were asked to estimate their score on a 10-point quiz. Their estimated and actual scores are given in the table below. Plot the points. Then sketch a line that fits the data.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis> Predicted</emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis> Actual</emphasis> 
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry> 6 </entry>
            <entry> 6 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 7 </entry>
            <entry> 7 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 7 </entry>
            <entry> 8 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 8 </entry>
            <entry> 8 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 7 </entry>
            <entry> 9 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 9 </entry>
            <entry> 10 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 10 </entry>
            <entry> 10 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 10 </entry>
            <entry> 9 </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <para>Here are the points from the table plotted on a scatter plot, with the \(x\)-axis being the predicted scores and the \(y\)-axis being the actual scores.</para>
      
        <media alt="A SCATTER PLOT THAT SHOWS PREDICTED SCORES ON THE X-AXIS AND ACTUAL SCORES ON THE Y-AXIS. THE LINE OF BEST FIT DRAWN INCREASES FROM LEFT TO RIGHT.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_03_08_02.jpg"/>
        </media>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Tell what you would expect someone’s grade to actually be if they predicted their grade to be a 5 out of 10.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Here is how to answer these questions using the linear model:</para>
          <para>Using the linear model above, if a student predicted a 5 on the quiz, they should have an actual score of 6.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What does the slope mean in this problem?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Here is how to answer these questions using the linear model:</para>
          <para>The slope is the change in the actual grade point for each predicted grade point.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    </section>
    
